#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e614d8e-5811-579d-b04a-473c177e6097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298550} {:answer/id #uuid "88a920aa-976c-5392-ab89-a8afb89dfead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88a920aa-976c-5392-ab89-a8afb89dfead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298551} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0248f05-f4bb-5916-9e44-4dee6f81dc37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afef5694-bf50-563b-a88c-5f1ccf492bde" :question/math-academy-id "q-352858" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-sin\\,(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff66d701-ccee-551c-96f2-ca7da2e776d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff66d701-ccee-551c-96f2-ca7da2e776d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298554} :answer-field/id #uuid "12018ba6-d93d-59f7-9f7e-13760e380cde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5555438-060c-5bd3-a19d-6d830dc51bce" :question/math-academy-id "q-352868" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8c3027e-65bf-5167-9725-2ee745184d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298557} {:answer/id #uuid "3191b9f4-02cf-5cd6-8864-c940680d0a30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298558} {:answer/id #uuid "81564b7e-7f70-5f3f-90b4-fefee4ac29cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{2}⁡3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298559} {:answer/id #uuid "51029c53-8df7-5703-8d0b-3beba0a8d5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298560} {:answer/id #uuid "00f92431-15b5-5ad4-8554-3d0397c3f125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8c3027e-65bf-5167-9725-2ee745184d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298557} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a6cac14-08de-54a8-8903-80c7cc92480a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc5d1374-23d8-5057-a23d-f12014c4a689" :question/math-academy-id "q-49256" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int {sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7af9f96-b157-5502-a3d1-cb60018b075c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin x - \\tan x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063155} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ea30f1-100f-5006-816a-5baf4e3bdd7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x - \\tan x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063156} {:answer/id #uuid "e2eaa5ac-e665-5654-94e1-cd38cab8fc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x - \\cot x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063157} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8b00e8-9f77-52c1-88c1-5a55d3abbdc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x + \\cot x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063158} {:answer/id #uuid "70c73302-07cb-510a-9359-39bd29e98228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x + \\tan x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2eaa5ac-e665-5654-94e1-cd38cab8fc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x - \\cot x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063157} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac2b109a-d1fc-50f0-b22a-55eef29d2a8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c589cab7-d081-524b-ae47-ab787d74dcb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49273" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that ${cot}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79cc335a-4d8c-5da6-ad35-fdb2e85ede4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298564} {:answer/id #uuid "8547d88d-cd17-5e08-8b5f-9c37767efb61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298565} {:answer/id #uuid "e5328206-8608-50a7-b967-6d143173523c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298566} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9ddddd-1a68-5d42-8c46-95452e1eb83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298567} {:answer/id #uuid "8b46762d-8b67-5739-b9b4-c9664dbc35d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79cc335a-4d8c-5da6-ad35-fdb2e85ede4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298564} :answer-field/id #uuid "28f018e5-6494-532a-819f-f7983465bd84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d84df214-b5ed-54c2-b781-28cd3f99db9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-49281" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡2x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡2x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let  $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
